A bakery method declares a tray count and prints it without ever giving that count a value. What does the compiler do with this program?

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int trays;
        System.out.println(trays);
    }
}

A bakery object declares a tray count as an instance field with no initialiser and prints it straight after construction. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    int trays;
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bakery k = new Bakery();
        System.out.println(k.trays);
    }
}

A bakery declares a temperature only inside a conditional block and then tries to read that name again after the block has closed. What does the compiler do with this program?

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (true) {
            int temp = 5;
            System.out.println(temp);
        }
        System.out.println(temp);
    }
}

A bakery prints a field through the this keyword from inside a static method. What does the compiler do with this program?

class Bakery {
    int trays;
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(this.trays);
    }
}

A bakery field is an ordinary instance field, but a static method tries to read it by bare name. Trace what happens to this program.

class Bakery {
    int trays = 4;
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(trays);
    }
}

A bakery declares a tray count final and then tries to give that count a new value in the next statement. Trace what happens to this program.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final int trays = 1;
        trays = 2;
        System.out.println(trays);
    }
}

A bakery reads a weight inside the catch clause, but the variable it names was declared inside the try clause. Trace what happens to this program.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            int kg = 1;
            throw new RuntimeException("x");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(kg);
        }
    }
}

A bakery loop runs twice and then tries to read its counter variable after the loop has finished. Trace what happens to this program.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) { }
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}

A bakery walks an array with an enhanced for loop and then tries to read the element variable after the loop has closed. Trace what happens to this program.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int v : new int[] { 1, 2 }) { }
        System.out.println(v);
    }
}

A bakery method declares a local that shares its name with a field, then prints the bare name and the this-qualified name. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    int trays = 1;
    void report() {
        int trays = 2;
        System.out.println(trays + " " + this.trays);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Bakery().report();
    }
}

A bakery developer redeclares a tray count inside a nested block, shadowing the local that is still in scope outside it. Trace what happens to this program.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int trays = 1;
        { int trays = 2; System.out.println(trays); }
    }
}

A bakery method uses two separate blocks and declares the same tray-count name inside each of them. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        { int trays = 1; System.out.println(trays); }
        { int trays = 2; System.out.println(trays); }
    }
}

A bakery method increments a field, and the program calls that method twice on the same object. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    int trays;
    void bake() { trays = trays + 1; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bakery k = new Bakery();
        k.bake();
        k.bake();
        System.out.println(k.trays);
    }
}

A bakery class keeps a tray count per object, and two separate objects are each given a different amount. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    int trays;
    void add(int n) { trays = trays + n; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bakery a = new Bakery();
        Bakery b = new Bakery();
        a.add(3);
        b.add(5);
        System.out.println(a.trays + " " + b.trays);
    }
}

A bakery method reassigns its parameter to a different value, and the caller prints its own variable afterwards. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    static void bake(int trays) { trays = 99; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int trays = 7;
        bake(trays);
        System.out.println(trays);
    }
}

A bakery program creates one object, points a second reference at it, and changes the field through the second name. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    int trays;
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bakery a = new Bakery();
        Bakery b = a;
        b.trays = 3;
        System.out.println(a.trays);
    }
}

A bakery field carries its own initialiser in the declaration, and a method prints it without touching it first. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    int trays = 7;
    void report() { System.out.println(trays); }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Bakery().report();
    }
}

A bakery class counts open ovens in a static field, and the program calls the opening method twice on one object. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    static int ovens;
    void open() { ovens = ovens + 1; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bakery a = new Bakery();
        a.open();
        a.open();
        System.out.println(Bakery.ovens);
    }
}

A bakery method declares a local that shadows a field, changes that local, and a second method then reads the field. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    int trays = 1;
    void report() { int trays = 9; System.out.println(trays); }
    void check() { System.out.println(trays); }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bakery k = new Bakery();
        k.report();
        k.check();
    }
}

A bakery oven class supplies its own toString method and the program prints one oven directly. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Oven {
    String id;
    Oven(String s) { id = s; }
    public String toString() { return "Oven(" + id + ")"; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Oven("OV-3"));
    }
}

A transit app joins an oven object onto a prefix string, relying on the description the object's own class supplies for itself. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Oven {
    public String toString() { return "Oven!"; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("v=" + new Oven());
    }
}

A bakery oven class declares no equals method, and the program compares two separately built ovens and then one oven with itself. Which pair of results does the program print?

class Oven {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Oven a = new Oven();
        Oven b = new Oven();
        System.out.println(a.equals(b));
        System.out.println(a.equals(a));
    }
}

A bakery oven class stores an identifier and declares no equals method, so two ovens built from the same identifier are compared directly. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Oven {
    String id;
    Oven(String s) { id = s; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Oven a = new Oven("OV-3");
        Oven b = new Oven("OV-3");
        System.out.println(a.equals(b));
    }
}

A bakery oven class overrides equals but not hashCode, and the program adds two ovens that carry the same identifier to a set. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Oven {
    String id;
    Oven(String s) { id = s; }
    public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Oven && ((Oven) o).id.equals(id); }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Set<Object> set = new java.util.HashSet<>();
        set.add(new Oven("OV-3"));
        set.add(new Oven("OV-3"));
        System.out.println(set.size());
    }
}

A bakery program holds one literal and one join of two literals, then compares the two names by reference. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "oven";
        String copy = "ov" + "en";
        System.out.println(name == copy);
    }
}

A bakery program holds one literal and one string built with the new operator, then compares the two names by reference. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "oven";
        String copy = new String("oven");
        System.out.println(name == copy);
    }
}

A bakery program holds one literal and one join whose operand is a variable, then compares the two names by reference. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Bakery {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String tag = "oven";
        String built = "oven";
        String joined = tag + "";
        System.out.println(built == joined);
    }
}

A transit route object can return a null for the next stop, and the program calls a method on whatever the chain hands back. Trace what happens to this program.

class Stop {
    Stop tail() { return null; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stop head = new Stop();
        System.out.println(head.tail().hashCode());
    }
}

A transit shift object accumulates a counter in two mutating methods, and the program reads the counter after each call inside a single addition. Trace the program and give the line it prints.

class Shift {
    int s;
    Shift a() { s = s + 1; return this; }
    Shift b() { s = s + 10; return this; }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shift x = new Shift();
        int r = x.a().s + x.b().s;
        System.out.println(r);
    }
}
